;;; 79-shape-stack.slat — Motoi Scheme Reference · Appendix / Chapter 79 ·
;;; The shape-math stack: the four ways Motoi turns numbers into pixels, and
;;; how they compose. A LEVEL-4 (analyze) chapter.
;;;
;;; Written 2026-07-29. This is an APPENDIX to the drawing libraries authored in
;;; chapters 73 (draw) · 74 (image) · 75 (image-compose) · 76 (superformula) ·
;;; 77 (sdf) · 78 (svg-path). Read those first for the per-verb records; read
;;; THIS to see the whole stack at once — what each layer is FOR, what math each
;;; one leans on, and the one seam where all four meet: the framebuffer.
;;;
;;; This chapter answers a level-4 question: given a shape you want, WHICH layer
;;; do you reach for, and why that one and not its neighbour? It is the map that
;;; sits above the six libraries. Every code block RUNS through ./bin/motoi
;;; (verified 2026-07-29); painting blocks are prefixed with (set-mode 'sd).
;;;
;;; Made by Alfred Borden.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "The shape-math stack — a level-4 analysis of the four drawing layers and their one shared seam.")

(chapter :number "79" :library "shape-stack" :kind "meta-chapter"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :author "Alfred Borden"
  :axis "sensation"
  :bloom "analyze"
  :read-order-hint "read after 73-draw through 78-svg-path; this is the map above them"
  :prose
"# The shape-math stack

Made by Alfred Borden.

## Four layers, one screen

Motoi draws in four different ways, and they are not four flavours of the same
thing — they are four different KINDS of math, each strongest at a shape the
others are clumsy with. Learning to draw in Motoi is mostly learning which of
the four to reach for. This chapter lays them side by side so the choice becomes
a diagnosis instead of a guess.

| layer | library | the math underneath | what it is best at |
|-------|---------|---------------------|--------------------|
| 1 · raster shapes | draw (73) | vertices + scanline fill | one shape, painted NOW, at fixed pixels |
| 2 · image algebra | image (74) · image-compose (75) | function composition / z-order | building a picture from parts, as a VALUE |
| 3 · superformula | superformula (76) | a polar function r(φ) sampled on a schedule | organic outlines — petals, shells, stars, blobs |
| 4 · signed distance | sdf (77) | min/max arithmetic on a distance field | boolean shapes — holes, unions, carved solids |
| (importer) · svg-path | svg-path (78) | a mini-language of pen moves → point list | reusing an existing icon or glyph |

The importer is set apart because it is not a fifth kind of math — it is a
FRONT DOOR onto layer 1. `svg-path->points` turns a path string into the same
point list `polygon` eats. So there are really four maths and one adaptor.

## Layer 1 — raster shapes: vertices, painted now

The raster layer is the floor of the stack. A `triangle` is three points and a
fill rule; a `polygon` is a closed point list; a `star` is inner and outer radii
alternating around a centre. These verbs PAINT — they change the framebuffer and
return nothing. There is no value to hold, no picture to pass around: the mark is
on the screen the instant the call returns.

That immediacy is the whole trade. Raster shapes are the fastest way to put a
known mark at known pixels, and the WORST way to build something you want to move,
scale, or reuse — because there is nothing left in your hand afterward. When you
find yourself painting the same shape at four positions with four calls, you have
outgrown layer 1 and want layer 2.

The math is a scanline: for each row of pixels the shape covers, find where the
edges cross and fill between them. Vertices in, filled span out. That is why the
fill verbs (`triangle-fill`, `polygon-fill`, `ngon-fill`) take the same arguments
as their outline twins — same geometry, different rule for what counts as inside.

## Layer 2 — image algebra: pictures as values

The image layer changes the noun. An `img/circle` does not paint — it RETURNS an
image, a value you can name, pass, and combine. `overlay` stacks images back-to-
front; `beside` sets them left-to-right; `above` stacks them top-to-bottom;
`rotate`, `scale`, and the `flip-*` pair transform one into another. Only at the
very end does `draw-image` commit the finished value to the framebuffer.

This is function composition wearing a picture's clothes. `(overlay a b)` is
`a ∘ b` in the z-order: the raster of the result asks A first, then B underneath.
Because images are values, the algebra is CLOSED — every combinator takes images
and returns an image, so you can nest them as deep as the picture needs. That
closure is the property layer 1 lacks and the reason a building made of nested
squares is one `overlay` expression here and four scattered paint calls there.

`image-width`, `image-height`, and `image?` are the layer's instruments: they let
you measure and test a picture without drawing it, which is how you assert an
invariant (a `beside` is exactly as wide as its parts summed) before a single
pixel is spent.

## Layer 3 — superformula: one equation, a garden of outlines

The superformula is a single polar equation — Johan Gielis's generalization of
the circle — that bends into petals, stars, shells, and blobs as you turn its
handful of knobs (m, n1, n2, n3, a, b). `superformula` samples that equation at a
schedule of angles and hands back a point list; `superformula-stroke` and
`superformula-fill` paint it.

The reason it earns its own layer is that it reaches shapes the other three can
only approximate. A polygon can fake a flower only by listing every vertex by
hand; the superformula GROWS the vertices from one equation, and a small change
to m turns a triangle into a pentagon into a twelve-lobed rosette without
touching the point list. It is the organic layer: when the shape you want has a
smooth, many-lobed, living outline, this is the only layer that describes it in a
handful of numbers instead of a hundred coordinates.

Because its output is a plain point list, it drops straight back down onto layer
1 — the points a superformula grows are the points a `polygon` fills. Layer 3
generates; layer 1 paints.

## Layer 4 — signed distance: shapes as arithmetic

An SDF (signed distance field) is not a set of points at all — it is a FUNCTION
that, for any pixel, returns its distance to the shape's edge (negative inside,
positive outside). `sdf/circle`, `sdf/box`, and `sdf/segment` build these
functions; `sdf-fill` evaluates one across the framebuffer and paints where the
distance is ≤ 0.

The payoff is that booleans become arithmetic. Union is `min` of two fields;
intersection is `max`; subtraction is `max(a, -b)`. That single fact is why the
SDF layer is the one to reach for whenever the shape has a HOLE or is CARVED — a
ring is a circle minus a smaller circle, a washer or a gear or a keyhole is a
handful of `sdf-subtract` calls. The other layers can outline such shapes but
cannot easily say what is inside a shape-with-a-bite-taken-out; the distance
field says it in one comparison.

The cost is that an SDF is evaluated per pixel, so it is the most expensive layer
per unit area — you pay for the generality. Reach for it when the boolean
structure earns the cost, not for a plain disc a raster `ellipse-fill` would draw
faster.

## The importer — svg-path: a front door onto layer 1

`svg-path->points` reads the same little language your vector editor writes: `M`
move, `L` line, `H`/`V` axis lines, `C` cubic curve, `Z` close. It returns a
point list. `draw-svg-path` and `fill-svg-path` paint it. This is not a new kind
of math — it is a PARSER that lands you back on layer 1 with a point list you did
not have to type. Its purpose is reuse: an icon someone already drew, a glyph, a
logo, brought in as coordinates instead of redrawn from scratch.

## The one seam: the framebuffer

Everything above meets at one place. Three of the four layers are PURE — image
algebra, superformula point lists, and SDF fields are all just values, and you
can build them with no framebuffer at all. They only touch the screen through a
SINK: `draw-image` for layer 2, `superformula-fill` for layer 3, `sdf-fill` for
layer 4, the raster verbs for layer 1. Every sink writes the same 320×240 SD
framebuffer, and every sink needs `(set-mode 'sd)` to have been called first.

That is the analytical heart of the stack. The four maths never talk to each
other in their own terms — a distance field knows nothing of a point list, an
image knows nothing of a polar equation. What they share is the destination.
Compose in whichever math fits the shape, then let each layer's sink pour onto
the one screen. A single frame can hold an overlaid image at 80,80, a
superformula stroke at 200,80, and a carved SDF at 160,180 — three different
maths, one framebuffer, because the seam is the pixel grid and nothing above it.

## How to choose — the level-4 diagnosis

Put the four side by side and the choice is almost mechanical:

- Want ONE mark at KNOWN pixels, right now? → **layer 1 (raster)**.
- Building a picture from PARTS you will move, scale, or reuse? → **layer 2 (image)**.
- Is the outline ORGANIC — petals, lobes, a smooth many-sided bloom? → **layer 3 (superformula)**.
- Does the shape have a HOLE or a BITE — a ring, a gear, a keyhole? → **layer 4 (sdf)**.
- Does the shape ALREADY EXIST as an icon or glyph? → **importer (svg-path)**.

When two layers could do it, the tie-breaker is what you need AFTERWARD. If you
need the shape as a reusable value, layer 2 wins even when layer 1 could paint it.
If you need to carve it, layer 4 wins even when layer 3 could outline it. The
shape tells you the math; what you do next tells you the layer."
)

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; Machine-readable layer records. Each carries the layer's identity, the math
;; underneath, and runnable programs (with expected outcome) that a fold
;; generator can extract and re-grade. Painting programs are prefixed with
;; (set-mode 'sd) and print nothing — their grade is "no error thrown".
;; Axis: sensation. Bloom: analyze.
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(stack-layer
  :id "1" :name "raster-shapes" :library "draw"
  :math "vertices + scanline fill"
  :returns "undefined (paints the framebuffer)"
  :best-at "one known mark at known pixels, immediately"
  :weak-at "reuse, movement, scaling — nothing is left in hand"
  :programs (
    (:code "(set-mode 'sd) (triangle-fill 10 10 40 10 25 40 8)" :expect ""
     :note "Three vertices, a fill rule, painted now. Nothing returned.")
    (:code "(set-mode 'sd) (star 60 60 20 8 5)" :expect ""
     :note "Inner/outer radii alternating around a centre — a raster star.")
    (:code "(define (tri-area x1 y1 x2 y2 x3 y3)\n  (abs (/ (+ (* x1 (- y2 y3)) (* x2 (- y3 y1)) (* x3 (- y1 y2))) 2)))\n(= (tri-area 0 0 4 0 0 3) 6)" :expect "#t"
     :note "The scanline fills the region a triangle bounds; its area is the shoelace determinant. Pure-math check, no paint."))
)

(stack-layer
  :id "2" :name "image-algebra" :library "image · image-compose"
  :math "function composition / z-order; a closed algebra of image values"
  :returns "an image VALUE (pure until draw-image)"
  :best-at "building a picture from reusable parts"
  :weak-at "nothing to add here beyond the cost of the extra value"
  :programs (
    (:code "(image? (overlay (img/square 8 'solid 7) (img/square 16 'outline 6) (img/square 24 'outline 5)))" :expect "#t"
     :note "overlay stacks back-to-front; the result is itself an image — the algebra is closed.")
    (:code "(image-width (beside (img/square 10 'solid 8) (img/square 10 'solid 12)))" :expect "20"
     :note "beside is exactly as wide as its parts summed — an invariant you can assert without painting.")
    (:code "(set-mode 'sd) (draw-image (overlay (img/square 10 'solid 7) (img/square 20 'outline 6) (img/square 30 'outline 5)) 100 100)" :expect ""
     :note "The whole nested-squares building committed to the framebuffer in ONE draw-image."))
)

(stack-layer
  :id "3" :name "superformula" :library "superformula"
  :math "polar function r(phi) (Gielis) sampled on an angle schedule"
  :returns "a point list (superformula) / undefined (stroke, fill)"
  :best-at "organic many-lobed outlines from a handful of knobs"
  :weak-at "sharp engineered shapes a polygon states more directly"
  :programs (
    (:code "(length (superformula 160 120 40 6 1 7 8))" :expect "180"
     :note "One equation, sampled at the default 180 angles, grows 180 vertices.")
    (:code "(define pts (superformula 160 120 40 0 1 1 1 1 1 180))\n(define cx (/ (apply + (map car pts)) (length pts)))\n(< (abs (- cx 160)) 0.001)" :expect "#t"
     :note "m=0 is the circle: its sampled centroid sits on the centre. A knob change (m) would bend it into lobes.")
    (:code "(set-mode 'sd) (superformula-fill 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 180 11)" :expect ""
     :note "The point list drops straight onto layer 1 to be filled — layer 3 generates, layer 1 paints."))
)

(stack-layer
  :id "4" :name "signed-distance" :library "sdf"
  :math "min/max arithmetic on a distance field; union=min, intersect=max, subtract=max(a,-b)"
  :returns "an sdf FUNCTION value (pure until sdf-fill)"
  :best-at "boolean shapes — holes, carves, unions"
  :weak-at "plain solids a raster fill draws faster; cost is per-pixel"
  :programs (
    (:code "(sdf? (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 100 100 30) (sdf/circle 100 100 15)))" :expect "#t"
     :note "A ring IS a circle minus a smaller circle — subtraction is max(a, -b), one comparison.")
    (:code "(let ((a (sdf/circle 100 100 20)) (b (sdf/box 100 100 15 15)))\n  (and (sdf? (sdf-union a b)) (sdf? (sdf-union b a))))" :expect "#t"
     :note "Union is min, and min is commutative — so sdf-union is too. The algebra is the arithmetic.")
    (:code "(set-mode 'sd) (sdf-fill (sdf-subtract (sdf/box 100 100 30 30) (sdf/circle 100 100 12)) 12)" :expect ""
     :note "A square with a round bite — the shape layer 1 cannot easily say is inside, the distance field says in one sign test."))
)

(stack-layer
  :id "importer" :name "svg-path" :library "svg-path"
  :math "a pen-move mini-language (M L H V C Z) parsed to a point list"
  :returns "a point list (svg-path->points) / undefined (draw, fill)"
  :best-at "reusing an icon or glyph that already exists"
  :weak-at "not a new math — a front door onto layer 1"
  :programs (
    (:code "(length (svg-path->points \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" 4 40 40))" :expect "4"
     :note "M/L/Z parsed into a closed point list — the same list polygon eats.")
    (:code "(set-mode 'sd) (fill-svg-path \"M12 2 L2 22 L22 22 Z\" 40 40 4 11)" :expect ""
     :note "An imported triangle filled — reuse, landing back on layer 1."))
)

(interop
  :name "one-framebuffer-seam"
  :claim "The four maths never share terms; they share only the destination pixel grid."
  :programs (
    (:code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(draw-image (overlay (img/circle 8 'solid 12) (img/circle 14 'outline 8)) 80 80)\n(superformula-stroke 200 80 30 6 1 7 8)\n(sdf-fill (sdf/circle 160 180 20) 11)" :expect ""
     :note "Layer 2, layer 3, and layer 4 pour onto ONE SD framebuffer in one program — three maths, one seam.")
    (:code "(image? (img/circle 10 'solid 'red))" :expect "#t"
     :note "Proof the value layers are pure: an image builds with no set-mode at all. Only the SINK needs the framebuffer."))
  :meta "Compose in whichever math fits the shape; each layer's sink commits to the same 320x240 SD grid. The seam is the pixel, and nothing above it."
)
